<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The Age of Sail</h1><p class="ws-lede">How small Atlantic kingdoms and a merchant republic built empires across the oceans.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/age-of-sail">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 5 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Treaty of Tordesillas</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Dutch Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Prince Henry the Navigator</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Vasco da Gama</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Portuguese Empire</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Slavery</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Sugar plantation</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Portuguese Empire</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;From the capture of Ceuta in 1415 to Goa, Malacca and Hormuz, Portugal built an empire of fortified trading posts rather than territory.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Spanish Empire</b>: &#8220;Columbus, Cortés and Pizarro made Spain master of the Americas, and under Philip II the first empire on which the sun never set.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 9, <b>British Empire</b>: &#8220;Built on a fiscal-military state and a professional Royal Navy, it became the largest empire in history, ruling roughly a quarter of the world's population.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;How small Atlantic kingdoms and a merchant republic built empires across the oceans.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/age-of-sail. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The Age of Sail</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Prince Henry the Navigator <span>born 1394 CE</span></li><li>Portuguese Empire <span>from 1415 CE</span></li><li>Vasco da Gama <span>born c. 1469 CE</span></li><li>Treaty of Tordesillas <span>1494</span></li><li>Dutch Empire <span>from 1581 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Slavery</dt><dd>Slavery is the condition in which people are treated as property and forced to work.</dd><dt>Sugar plantation</dt><dd>A sugar plantation is a large agricultural estate growing sugarcane for sugar production, typically using intensive labour.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
